\tag{fdip}
\title{Preface}
\date{2026-11-04}


\p{Physics often models length, time and field values as continuous,
infinitely divisible quantities. Measurements have finite resolution, so they
do not establish that this continuum is fundamental; the real numbers are its
usual mathematical form. The hypothesis behind these notes is that geometry,
time and matter can be induced from finite carriers and exact algebra, with
continuum physics recovered at large scales. Induced gravity is one reason to take
it seriously: on a fixed background, one-loop matter fluctuations
generate an Einstein-Hilbert term in the effective action
\citet{Section 1, Eq. (1)}{visser2002sakharov}, so the gravitational action
need not be assumed. The alternating four-cycle of \ref{fdip-0003} is the
object that carries the weights and half-turns in their examples. How much of a continuum can be built from finitely many
points, and what has to be supplied instead?}

\p{The discrete setting is the one lattice field theory already provides: a finite
incidence presentation, fields valued in a commutative ring, finite matrices for the
operators, and Euclidean time on a lattice. Selected backgrounds, gauge fields,
Euclidean time and lattice fermions give the finite constructions that the
structure chapters and the closing \ref{fdip-003W} compare; the induced-gravity
literature is where those finite constructions are asked to generate the continuum
action.}

\p{No statement in these notes fixes a spacetime, a physical parameter, a
Standard Model equation or a gravitational equation.}

